      "worked": "Since $\\sqrt{101}<11$, the only primes to test are $2,3,5,7$. None divides $101$ (in particular, $101=7\\times14+3$), so $101$ is prime.\n\nLet $n$ be a term containing $k>2$ ones. Multiplying by $100$ and subtracting $n$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}99n&=10^{2k}-1\\\\99n&=(10^k-1)(10^k+1).\\end{align*}$$\nSuppose $n$ is prime. Since $n$ divides this product, it divides at least one factor. If $n\\mid(10^k-1)$, then\n$$\\frac{10^k-1}{n}=\\frac{99}{10^k+1}.$$\nIf $n\\mid(10^k+1)$, then\n$$\\frac{10^k+1}{n}=\\frac{99}{10^k-1}.$$\nIn either case, the left side is a positive integer, but the right side is strictly between $0$ and $1$, because $k>2$. This is a contradiction. Every term after $101$ is therefore composite, so the sequence contains precisely one prime.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
